# How much work buys an hour of light, then and now?
subtitle: "In 1800 an hour of bulb-bright light cost over five hours of work. Guess what it costs now, and see light become history's great bargain."
tags: ["economics","history","data"]
format: quiz
thumb: "band \"The bargain of the millennium\" note \"1800: candles\""
voices: {"a":"gemini:Callirrhoe | an easy-going quiz host; warm and relaxed","take":"lines"}

## How much work buys an hour of light, then and now?
text: {"font_size":30}
sources:
    - id: owid
      title: Hours of work required to produce 1,000 lumen-hours
      authors: Our World in Data, from William Nordhaus (1996)
      year: 2024
      url: https://ourworldindata.org/grapher/hours-of-work-per-1000-lumen-hours
      finding: '1,000 lumen-hours, about an hour of an 18-watt low-energy bulb: 5.37 hours of work in 1800, 0.326 in 1875, 0.012 in 1920, 0.000119 (about 0.43 seconds) in 1992'
    - id: nordhaus
      title: Do real-output and real-wage measures capture reality? The history of lighting suggests not
      authors: William D. Nordhaus
      year: 1996
      finding: the true price of light fell far faster than price indexes, which priced lamps and fuels, recorded
    - id: fouquet
      title: The price of lighting has dropped over 99.9% since 1700
      authors: Esteban Ortiz-Ospina and Pablo Arriagada, Our World in Data, from Roger Fouquet
      year: 2026
      url: https://ourworldindata.org/data-insights/the-price-of-lighting-has-dropped-over-999-since-1700
      finding: in the UK, a million lumen-hours cost about £34,000 in the early 1300s and £2.15 in 2023 (2000 prices)

