    text b_lab "1992: ~350 days" #2f6b8f cites ["owid"] x 730 y 285 font_size 46 hidden true
    text one_h "1 hour of work buys…" #3d3833 x 500 y 640 font_size 46 hidden true

    hide light_marker_2 light_marker_3

    card title "An hour of light: how much work?"

How long must you work to pay for an hour of light, the glow of one low-energy bulb?
    draw bulb hour

In 1800, by candlelight, it cost over five hours of work. Each step on this line is ten times the last.
    draw candle light

    ask "Click the line." on light store g default "60" keep true
        right: Good guess. Barely any work at all.
        wrong: Not quite. Each step down the line is ten times less work.

By 1992, less than half a second of work, for the same hour of light.
    highlight light_answer_pin light_answer_num effect circle

On the way down, kerosene lamps cut it to about twenty minutes by the 1870s,
    draw light_marker_2

and electric bulbs to under a minute by 1920.
    draw light_marker_3

Five hours to half a second: about forty-five thousand times less work.
    draw ratio

    erase light bulb candle hour ratio

Turn it around. In 1800, an hour of work bought about eleven minutes of candlelight.
    draw one_h c_icon c_face c_slice c_lab

By 1992, the same hour bought a bulb burning day and night for most of a year.
    draw b_icon b_face b_slice b_lab

And price statistics missed most of it: they tracked candles and lamps, not light itself, as the economist William Nordhaus showed.
    highlight c_slice

So the light your ancestors saved candle stubs for had become almost free.
    highlight b_slice

